A||N||6||bcwawright||Intake Plenum-Paint or Polish||||5||
Z||000000||bcwawright||05-18-2001||02:17 PM||||My intake plenum looks bad. If I repaint what is the best paint to use that will hold up under te heat.  Also is there someone who polishes the upper plenum to a bright finish if I decide not to paint?<P>Bruce<BR>88GT Turbo Spyder F355||||reg||5
Z||000001||NASAReeves||05-18-2001||02:46 PM||||I recently took mine off & refinished it.  First, I cleaned all of the vacuum hose areas well with Air Intake Cleaner.  <P>Next, I sanded the plenum down well.  I primed it, and repainted it with 500 degree Universal Red (from Advanced Auto).  This was virtually an exact match.  I let it bake in the sun for a day, put a second coat on, and let it bake some more.  Then, I covered it with a clear-coat.<P>After all was dry, I took the dremel, and sanded the paint away from "Fiero," and the silver bars on the top.  Lastly, I put the polish tool on the dremel, and used some Mother's (also from Advanced Auto) to polish the Aluminum.  Looks like a million bucks.<P>------------------<BR>Scott & Traci<BR>'88 GT||||reg||3
Z||000002||Cooter||05-18-2001||03:24 PM||||The intakes are very rough cast and porous castings. It would take a while to get it smooth enough to look good polished or plated, but it can be done. Regular high temp engine paint will hold up fine on the intake.||||reg||1
Z||000003||bcwawright||05-18-2001||03:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by NASAReeves:<BR><B>I recently took mine off & refinished it.  First, I cleaned all of the vacuum hose areas well with Air Intake Cleaner.  <P>Next, I sanded the plenum down well.  I primed it, and repainted it with 500 degree Universal Red (from Advanced Auto).  This was virtually an exact match.  I let it bake in the sun for a day, put a second coat on, and let it bake some more.  Then, I covered it with a clear-coat.<P>After all was dry, I took the dremel, and sanded the paint away from "Fiero," and the silver bars on the top.  Lastly, I put the polish tool on the dremel, and used some Mother's (also from Advanced Auto) to polish the Aluminum.  Looks like a million bucks.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks!! I'm off to see the Wizard(oops..I mean Advanced Auto).  I'm going to bake mine in the oven....my wife will love that.<P>Bruce 88GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||spideyfoot||05-18-2001||05:46 PM||||I dont think I would cook em in the oven if I was you. Everything Ive ever seen says if you are gonna cure something like paint/powdercoat you need a seperate cheap oven in your shop. Besides it will be plenty cured when sprayed from the can and a little time. You either need to shoot a light coat every 15 minutes and a few gloss coat every 30 or wait for a complete cure between coats I rec.  the first. Put your rattle can in hot water to help the paint flow and start off with some real light mist coats. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||bcwawright||05-18-2001||07:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by spideyfoot:<BR><B>I dont think I would cook em in the oven if I was you. Everything Ive ever seen says if you are gonna cure something like paint/powdercoat you need a seperate cheap oven in your shop. Besides it will be plenty cured when sprayed from the can and a little time. You either need to shoot a light coat every 15 minutes and a few gloss coat every 30 or wait for a complete cure between coats I rec.  the first. Put your rattle can in hot water to help the paint flow and start off with some real light mist coats. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the tips.<P>Bruce<BR>88GT <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||05-19-2001||12:14 AM||||Of the plenums that I have seen polished or chromed I have never been able to really like it. The Fiero engraving essentially disappears and even if you have other things polished it just looks like a great big blob of chrome. It just overwhelms the engine compartment and looks really tacky. If you want to make a statement paint the plenum a different color. <P>The colors I have seen have been a HUGE difference in the engine bay! The first one I saw was a yellow plenum in a yellow GT (Chris Sideris multiple Best of Show winner) Orange in an Orange SE (Keith Huff's also an award winner) and white (man it looked really brutal to keep clean!) I took mine and took off the casting flash and had it powdercoated and with the top and neck polished. ||||reg||14
